      {
        "id": "s3_parts",
        "kind": "boxed_final",
        "label": "Parts from outside in",
        "marks": 1,
        "mark_note": "Parts",
        "lines": [
          {
            "text": "Lens, crystalline cone, rhabdom, basement membrane",
            "style": "boxed"
          },
          "The cornea, or lens, is the outer transparent",
          "facet. Two corneagen cells below it secrete",
          "the lens.",
          "Four cone cells below them form the",
          "crystalline cone, and an iris pigment sheath",
          "surrounds it.",
          "Seven or eight retinular cells lie below the",
          "cone. Their inner edges form the rod-shaped",
          "rhabdom, which takes in the light.",
          "A retinular pigment sheath surrounds these",
          "cells and keeps the light of one ommatidium",
          "from reaching the next.",
          "Nerve fibres leave through the basement",
          "membrane at the base."
        ],
        "why": "Listing the parts in the order the light passes them turns nine labels into one path, and it explains what each part is for instead of leaving them as names.",
        "common_mistakes": [
          "Writing that the cone cells make the lens. The corneagen cells secrete the lens; the cone cells make the crystalline cone.",
          "Writing that the pigment sheaths absorb the image. They stop light crossing between neighbouring ommatidia."
        ],
        "memory_tip": "Follow the light down: lens, cone, rhabdom, nerve. Pigment sheaths run alongside the whole way.",
        "margin_note": "One line per part, top to bottom in the order the light travels. Five sentences for this mark."
      }
    ]
  },
  "insider_note": "This is a figure question, so the labels carry the credit — the rhabdom drawn inside the ring of retinular cells and the basement membrane at the base are the two labels students most often lose."
